    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, im studying for my security+ and im stuck on one thing. if we are already hashing passwords, why do we even need to add a \"salt\" to them? wouldnt the hash alone be enough to keep it secure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Hashing alone is insufficient because identical passwords produce identical hashes, making them vulnerable to rainbow table attacks. A salt is a unique, random string added to a password *before* it is hashed. This ensures that even if two users choose the same password, their stored hashes will be completely different, thwarting pre-computed dictionary and rainbow table attacks.",
