    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok i get that. but how does the salt actually stop the rainbow table though? if an attacker just adds the salt to their list of guesses, wouldn't it just be the same thing? can you explain the specific mechanism of how it breaks the pre-computed part of the attack?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Rainbow tables rely on pre-calculating hashes for every possible input password. By adding a unique salt to each password, the attacker cannot use a single pre-computed table. They would have to generate and store a massive, unique rainbow table for *every single salt* used in your system. This massive, personalized computation is computationally infeasible for an attacker in a practical timeframe.",
      "source": "target",
