    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so it basically just ruins the efficiency of the attack. is there anything else besides salting that we use to make the hashing process even harder or slower for an attacker to brute force? i think i heard something about \"stretching\" or something like that in my notes.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, \"stretching,\" which involves using slow, intentional computational difficulty, is the next crucial step, often implemented via Key Derivation Functions (KDFs) like Argon2 or bcrypt. These functions repeatedly run the hashing algorithm thousands or millions of times. This intentional slowdown dramatically increases the attacker's cost per guess, as each attempt requires significant CPU time instead of a near-instantaneous calculation. This combination of salting and stretching ensures that even if an attacker has a hash, cracking it is prohibitively expensive.",
      "source": "target",
